# Next steps

## Manage transformation of memtables into sstables

I need to keep track of existing memtables, sstables and their status.
memtable status: ACTIVE, PERSISTING, PERSISTED, DELETED
sstable status: CREATING, ACTIVE, MERGING, MERGED, DELETED

There should only be 1 ACTIVE memtable at a time.
Writes are performed against the ACTIVE memtable.
Lookups are performed against ACTIVE and PERSISTING memtables, and then against
ACTIVE and MERGING SSTables.
Upon a 1MB size threshold, memtable should go from ACTIVE to PERSISTING and a new
ACTIVE memtable should be created.
PERSISTING should happen a background thread. Once PERSISTING finishes, status
changes to PERSISTED.
When status of the memtable changes to PERSISTED, the corresponding SSTable should
change from CREATING to ACTIVE atomically.
From time to time, compaction will occur, picking two SSTables and changing their
status to MERGING. This compaction will result in a new SSTable in status CREATING.
When compaction ends, the original SSTables should go from MERGING to MERGED
atomically and the new SSTable should go from CREATING to ACTIVE.

A WAL needs to exist to track these changes in the LSM structure.
Creating ACTIVE memtable appends to the WAL with action CREATING-MEMTABLE {id}, and then CREATED-MEMTABLE {id}
Persisting memtable appends to WAL with action PERSISTING-MEMTABLE {id}.
When persisting ends, it's written to WAL PERSISTED-MEMTABLE {id}.
This shold be followed by DELETED-MEMTABLE-WAL {id} once the WAL is deleted.

When recovering the LSM Tree, CREATING-MEMTABLE {id} means we should recreate the 
last memtable.
When recovering the LSM Tree, PERSISTING-MEMTABLE {id} means we should restart the persisting of memtable. Memtable should be loaded as PERSISTING.
When recovering the LSM Tree, PERSISTED-MEMTABLE {id} means we delete the WAL associated with that memtable. Memtable {id} does not need to be loaded. SSTable {id} should be loaded.
When recovering the LSM Tree, DELETED-MEMTABLE {id} means we should load SSTable {id} as ACTIVE.

Starting compaction of SSTables should append MERGING-SSTABLE {id1} {id2} {new-id}.
Finishing compaction should append MERGED-SSTABLE {id1} {id2} {new-id}.
Deleting SSTable should append DELETED-SSTABLE {id}

When recovering the LSM Tree, MERGING-SSTABLE {id1} {id2} {new-id} should restart the merging process. {id1} and {id2} should be loaded as 

LSM Tree state
MemTables: {id, MemTableStatus}
SSTable: {id, SSTableStatus}